EXT. LAKESIDE BOATHOUSE - DUSK

A weathered boathouse leaning over green water. Paper lanterns hang from the rafters, half of them unlit. A phonograph on an upturned crate turns a record, scratchy and slow.

THEODORA "TEDDY" LUND, 30s, in a yellow dress that has seen better summers, sits on the dock with her feet in the water. Next to her, ARMAND BELLWETHER, 30s, a rower's shoulders under a rumpled linen shirt, holds two glasses and a bottle without a label. Behind them on the boathouse porch stand her sister CORINNE, 40s, arms folded, and Armand's father OLD MR. BELLWETHER, 70s, in a cardigan, who has said nothing since they arrived.

TEDDY
Armand, you're going to drop it.

ARMAND
I'm not going to drop it.

TEDDY
You're holding it like a baby.

ARMAND
A baby wouldn't have a cork.

CORINNE
Teddy. Dinner.

TEDDY
In a minute, Cor.

CORINNE
You said that an hour ago.

TEDDY
I meant it then too.

Armand pours. The wine is pale. They clink. Old Mr. Bellwether coughs once and turns toward the water.

ARMAND
Father, you want some?

OLD MR. BELLWETHER
I want to know why my son is sitting on a dock in a good shirt.

ARMAND
Because I've been meaning to ask her something.

TEDDY
(smiling)
Then ask.

He sets his glass down. A dragonfly lands on its rim. Corinne's arms unfold.
